#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623792 :question/problem "![](images/3d/3dcd48f167c1abd8135af4802bfe3c6abd980eba1ef2bbf0adb87156d7c2faa3.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,-3)$ and $(1,-4)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/93/930d1bbba272eb5b747ebc4ece3a2b93e7509cbd1e0b58903096149854319288.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=-x-3.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-3)$ and $B(1,-4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3) &=-(0)-3 \\\\ -3 &=-3\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-4) &=-(1)-3 \\\\ -4 &=-4\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-x-3$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $2y+2x=-6.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-3)$ and $B(1,-4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-3)+2(0) &=-6 \\\\ -6 &=-6\\,\\checkmark \\\\ 2(-4)+2(1) &=-6 \\\\ -6 &=-6\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2y+2x=-6$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=-3+x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-3)$ and $B(1,-4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3) &=-3+(0) \\\\ -3 &=-3\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-4) &=-3+(1) \\\\ -4 &=-2\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-3+x$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nSelect $y=-x-3$, $2y+2x=-6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623805 :question/problem "![](images/76/765f4148294d78ace114858bfcd459d1eef17dd7cfcd54764b7b848b5e6acbf2.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,4)$ and $(1,6)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/16/162d47dcc98dfa30f90d511692061b524b96cac0fefa87e5db478d5cbd859b78.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=2x+5.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) &=2(0)+5 \\\\ 4 &=5\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2x+5$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=4+2x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(1,6)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) &=4+2(0) \\\\ 4 &=4\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (6) &=4+2(1) \\\\ 6 &=6\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=4+2x$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y-2x=4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(1,6)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4)-2(0) &=4 \\\\ 4 &=4\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (6)-2(1) &=4 \\\\ 4 &=4\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y-2x=4$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nSelect $y=4+2x$, $y-2x=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623822 :question/problem "![](images/da/da52fbd751e778bc191d6a7067d8bf0de81b46a1eb7db10a8af0de965c735c0e.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,-1)$ and $(1,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/6c/6c23998a9dfa5ca9c3a2cfe12eafd08f2382f6006b284b04181d56c902f6c6eb.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=-x-1.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(1,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) &=-(0)-1 \\\\ -1 &=-1\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (0) &=-(1)-1 \\\\ 0 &=-2\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-x-1$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $2y-2x=-2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(1,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-1)-2(0) &=-2 \\\\ -2 &=-2\\,\\checkmark \\\\ 2(0)-2(1) &=-2 \\\\ -2 &=-2\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2y-2x=-2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=-1-x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(1,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) &=-1-(0) \\\\ -1 &=-1\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (0) &=-1-(1) \\\\ 0 &=-2\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-1-x$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nSelect $2y-2x=-2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623832 :question/problem "![](images/80/8088eeb04d86e5719ce7b3d9f62a17a97c012107d1bff7acd9fa37c6b473ca3e.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,2)$ and $(1,3)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/d8/d8cbae503bf244e18d3ac62bc4ef87cc775e71b68e5bdab888a3c776d5080090.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(1,3)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) &=(0)+2 \\\\ 2 &=2\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (3) &=(1)+2 \\\\ 3 &=3\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=x+2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=2+x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(1,3)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) &=2+(0) \\\\ 2 &=2\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (3) &=2+(1) \\\\ 3 &=3\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2+x$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y-x=2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(1,3)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)-(0) &=2 \\\\ 2 &=2\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (3)-(1) &=2 \\\\ 2 &=2\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y-x=2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nSelect $y=x+2$, $y=2+x$, $y-x=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624510 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=3{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x{y}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=6x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=6x+(-6y)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does have a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2+(-2)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624526 :question/problem "![](images/8e/8ec6c3353156edf794d0da25ccdebb8421a2570a6ec81625c54affcafe303889.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Which of the following shows the streamlines of this vector field?" :question/worked-solution "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nOf the given options, the only level curves tangential to the vector field at every point are shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1bbd13918b2cda87c82eb7c9e7cc33cff46642d94725a806df23a94da6b0d5e8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624733 :question/problem "![](images/55/55a0c85ae8e31f1186047e306f0db40009659c63e38f2b0787ad500d3e7852d7.png)\n\nLet $C(-1,5)$ and $D(1,3)$. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{CD}|$." :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=- i + 5 j, \\\\ & \\overset{\\to }{OD}=d=i + 3 j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD} &= \\overset{\\to }{OD}-\\overset{\\to }{OC} \\\\ &= d-c \\\\ &= (i + 3 j)-(- i + 5 j) \\\\ &= 2 i - 2 j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{CD}| &= \\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{4+4} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{8} \\\\ &= 2 \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624750 :question/problem "![](images/a2/a281dc0c074186c13f2a961db2e6df2be8d25404ab1ec299cef6f2a01c3b2b9e.png)\n\nLet $C(-2,-5)$ and $D(5,1)$. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{CD}|$." :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=- 2 i - 5 j, \\\\ & \\overset{\\to }{OD}=d=5 i + j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD} &= \\overset{\\to }{OD}-\\overset{\\to }{OC} \\\\ &= d-c \\\\ &= (5 i + j)-(- 2 i - 5 j) \\\\ &= 7 i + 6 j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{CD}| &= \\sqrt{(7)^{2}+(6)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{49+36} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{85}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624757 :question/problem "![](images/05/0549ea9d87eeb598bd42682b411e6d7207d1c82a671901e5060ec57e621fdf7e.png)\n\nLet $A(-2,5)$ and $B(-5,1)$. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{AB}|$. Do not round the answer.\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{AB}|=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=- 2 i + 5 j, \\\\ & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=- 5 i + j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} &= \\overset{\\to }{OB}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\ &= b-a \\\\ &= (- 5 i + j)-(- 2 i + 5 j) \\\\ &= - 3 i - 4 j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| &= \\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{9+16} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{25} \\\\ &= 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624758 :question/problem "![](images/41/416e1a45bc4afe4086f3b4fab9aa0a5f48d51724d5e06699b37052f21f6866cc.png)\n\nLet $P(-4,-2)$ and $Q(3,5)$. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}|$." :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OP}=p=- 4 i - 2 j, \\\\ & \\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q=3 i + 5 j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} &= \\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\ &= q-p \\\\ &= (3 i + 5 j)-(- 4 i - 2 j) \\\\ &= 7 i + 7 j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| &= \\sqrt{(7)^{2}+(7)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{49+49} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{98} \\\\ &= 7 \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624761 :question/problem "![](images/a9/a95768bff785b622ec4f69bea1361b7d0bf02f721fd6e3f86efbf6d616a9e367.png)\n\nLet $C(-2,0)$ and $D(0,-4)$. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{CD}|$." :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=- 2 i, \\\\ & \\overset{\\to }{OD}=d=- 4 j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD} &= \\overset{\\to }{OD}-\\overset{\\to }{OC} \\\\ &= d-c \\\\ &= (- 4 j)-(- 2 i) \\\\ &= 2 i - 4 j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{CD}| &= \\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{4+16} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{20} \\\\ &= 2 \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624766 :question/problem "Let $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $b$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{b}{|b|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|b| &= \\sqrt{(-6)^2+(7)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{85} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $b$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{b}{|b|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{85}}\\cdot (b) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{85}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{6 \\sqrt{85}}{85} \\\\ \\frac{7 \\sqrt{85}}{85}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624778 :question/problem "Let $n=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $n$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{n}{|n|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|n| &= \\sqrt{(-10)^2+(-5)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{125} \\\\\n&= 5 \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $n$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{n}{|n|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5 \\sqrt{5}}\\cdot (n) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5 \\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624781 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{a}{|a|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a| &= \\sqrt{(-5)^2+(3)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{34} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{34}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $a$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{a}{|a|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}\\cdot (a) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{5 \\sqrt{34}}{34} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{34}}{34}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624792 :question/problem "Let $b=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $b$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{b}{|b|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|b| &= \\sqrt{(9)^2+(10)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{181} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{181}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $b$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{b}{|b|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{181}}\\cdot (b) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{181}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9 \\sqrt{181}}{181} \\\\ \\frac{10 \\sqrt{181}}{181}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624796 :question/problem "Let $n=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $n$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{n}{|n|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|n| &= \\sqrt{(12)^2+(-9)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{225} \\\\\n&= 15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $n$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{n}{|n|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{15}\\cdot (n) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{15}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624810 :question/problem "![](images/4f/4fac094a684d7209071c624ffe184184d34d3b3439ade505555c5ecb1cbc2a02.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,4)$ and $(2,3)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/92/92b2d2e714fce68ca3b8a38d1ba35d8b35971d3bbb2a238d7d68f48042b8aa3c.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=- \\frac{1}{2}x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(2,3)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) &=- \\frac{1}{2}(0)+4 \\\\ 4 &=4\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (3) &=- \\frac{1}{2}(2)+4 \\\\ 3 &=3\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=- \\frac{1}{2}x+4$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=4-2x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(2,3)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) &=4-2(0) \\\\ 4 &=4\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (3) &=4-2(2) \\\\ 3 &=0\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=4-2x$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y+\\frac{1}{2}x=4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(2,3)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4)+\\frac{1}{2}(0) &=4 \\\\ 4 &=4\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (3)+\\frac{1}{2}(2) &=4 \\\\ 4 &=4\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y+\\frac{1}{2}x=4$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nSelect $y=- \\frac{1}{2}x+4$, $y+\\frac{1}{2}x=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624828 :question/problem "![](images/ef/efe35f51fa4ce8707e2ac4ce1785ece55e9191bcedbe2b22ff64b5e4510eb5c7.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,-2)$ and $(2,-1)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/ac/ace116847d9b31abeb947a1e3952dc9f6aea7224602d394d9318efc51476bc6e.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-2)$ and $B(2,-1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2) &=\\frac{1}{2}(0)-2 \\\\ -2 &=-2\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-1) &=\\frac{1}{2}(2)-2 \\\\ -1 &=-1\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=-2+\\frac{1}{2}x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-2)$ and $B(2,-1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2) &=-2+\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\ -2 &=-2\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-1) &=-2+\\frac{1}{2}(2) \\\\ -1 &=-1\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2+\\frac{1}{2}x$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y-2x=-2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-2)$ and $B(2,-1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)-2(0) &=-2 \\\\ -2 &=-2\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-1)-2(2) &=-2 \\\\ -5 &=-2\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y-2x=-2$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nSelect $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-2$, $y=-2+\\frac{1}{2}x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624845 :question/problem "![](images/77/776ef945cb188befda6d6479bf10120428e9b77efcd4634c9bec02ecf4134cfc.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,1)$ and $(3,-1)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/2f/2f534e895418435f613e47853d84c692fce0281cd1f11bfe4779c84d33712f46.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+1.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,1)$ and $B(3,-1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1) &=\\frac{2}{3}(0)+1 \\\\ 1 &=1\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-1) &=\\frac{2}{3}(3)+1 \\\\ -1 &=3\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+1$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $2y-\\frac{4}{3}x=2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,1)$ and $B(3,-1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)-\\frac{4}{3}(0) &=2 \\\\ 2 &=2\\,\\checkmark \\\\ 2(-1)-\\frac{4}{3}(3) &=2 \\\\ -6 &=2\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2y-\\frac{4}{3}x=2$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=1-\\frac{2}{3}x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,1)$ and $B(3,-1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1) &=1-\\frac{2}{3}(0) \\\\ 1 &=1\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-1) &=1-\\frac{2}{3}(3) \\\\ -1 &=-1\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=1-\\frac{2}{3}x$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nSelect $y=1-\\frac{2}{3}x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624848 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a49ee497281145f15b2ba053557e1f783ec98f9cd41f83a8d77ec3ddf8c60813.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,-1)$ and $(2,2)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/11/11926789df1b1240bb41a88bddc7fea03f81e1f23f813add7de979b3afe777fa.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=\\frac{2}{3}x-1.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(2,2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) &=\\frac{2}{3}(0)-1 \\\\ -1 &=-1\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (2) &=\\frac{2}{3}(2)-1 \\\\ 2 &=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{2}{3}x-1$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $2y-3x=-2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(2,2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-1)-3(0) &=-2 \\\\ -2 &=-2\\,\\checkmark \\\\ 2(2)-3(2) &=-2 \\\\ -2 &=-2\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2y-3x=-2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=-1+\\frac{3}{2}x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(2,2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) &=-1+\\frac{3}{2}(0) \\\\ -1 &=-1\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (2) &=-1+\\frac{3}{2}(2) \\\\ 2 &=2\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-1+\\frac{3}{2}x$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nSelect $2y-3x=-2$, $y=-1+\\frac{3}{2}x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624849 :question/problem "![](images/58/58fe55d2d673fb6ad2335444e6b704a7139b591304ee4fa883bfd37adb295661.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,-3)$ and $(2,-6)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/75/75097de1c2a2d54e75ddbc626f01da74ad6fd21bc6d1d57eb5bc4b06a6fd8bcc.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=- \\frac{3}{2}x-3.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-3)$ and $B(2,-6)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3) &=- \\frac{3}{2}(0)-3 \\\\ -3 &=-3\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-6) &=- \\frac{3}{2}(2)-3 \\\\ -6 &=-6\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=- \\frac{3}{2}x-3$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=-3-\\frac{2}{3}x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-3)$ and $B(2,-6)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3) &=-3-\\frac{2}{3}(0) \\\\ -3 &=-3\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-6) &=-3-\\frac{2}{3}(2) \\\\ -6 &=- \\frac{13}{3}\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-3-\\frac{2}{3}x$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y+\\frac{2}{3}x=-3.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-3)$ and $B(2,-6)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)+\\frac{2}{3}(0) &=-3 \\\\ -3 &=-3\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-6)+\\frac{2}{3}(2) &=-3 \\\\ - \\frac{14}{3} &=-3\\,\\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y+\\frac{2}{3}x=-3$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nSelect $y=- \\frac{3}{2}x-3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625840 :question/problem "The inequality $-8<x<-3$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"less than\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-8$ and $-3$ are *not* included. \n\n$$\n-8\\,\\,<\\,\\,x\\,\\,<\\,\\,-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n(-8,-3)\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (-8,-3).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625845 :question/problem "The inequality $-12 < x < -5$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"less than\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-12$ and $-5$ are *not* included. \n\n$$\n-12\\,\\,<\\,\\,x\\,\\,<\\,\\,-5\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n(-12,-5)\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (-12,-5).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625848 :question/problem "The inequality $-\\sqrt{6}<x<\\sqrt{6}$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"less than\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-\\sqrt{6}$ and $\\sqrt{6}$ are *not* included. \n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{6}\\,\\,<\\,\\,x\\,\\,<\\,\\,\\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n(-\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{6})\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{6}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625858 :question/problem "The inequality $7 < x < 12$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"less than\" symbols. So, the endpoints $7$ and $12$ are *not* included. \n\n$$\n7\\,\\,<\\,\\,x\\,\\,<\\,\\,12\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n(7,12)\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (7,12).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625863 :question/problem "The inequality $-\\sqrt{3}<x<\\sqrt{3}$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"less than\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$ are *not* included. \n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\,<\\,\\,x\\,\\,<\\,\\,\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n(-\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{3})\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{3}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625874 :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b61d9efc26ed26f54086403a44647b215ac1a2068ba0db478ea3395841f9385.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-4\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, -2\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}-4,-2{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625876 :question/problem "![](images/61/6148116755d94b0527eeb1fe4d37b738f44e9dab561b3bb46e4303cd708acc94.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, 5\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}-1,5{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625890 :question/problem "![](images/d2/d23a7bd2127827593294eea4eb3e52323b05148afb53aad207a162cb7cb6b525.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n5\\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, 6\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{red}{(}}5,6{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625898 :question/problem "![](images/53/53943917d36ccad70981deb3228852e7c651ef187423e9b54455e906a885ed7c.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-4\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, 1\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}-4,1{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625900 :question/problem "![](images/0d/0d34c5a9f91b07044bc72c15b717f02cf1dcf151ab216c6192661c88fe6f8fc2.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-1\\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, 3\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{red}{(}}-1,3{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625910 :question/problem "The inequality $9\\ge y\\ge -6$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-6$ and $9$ *are* included. \n\n$$\n9\n\\,\\,\\ge \\,\\,y\\,\\,\\ge \\,\\,\n-6\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-6,9]\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\ny\\in [-6,9].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625911 :question/problem "The inequality $7\\ge t\\ge -12$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-12$ and $7$ *are* included. \n\n$$\n7\n\\,\\,\\ge \\,\\,t\\,\\,\\ge \\,\\,\n-12\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-12,7]\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nt\\in [-12,7].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625916 :question/problem "The inequality $2\\ge t\\ge -12$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-12$ and $2$ *are* included. \n\n$$\n2\n\\,\\,\\ge \\,\\,t\\,\\,\\ge \\,\\,\n-12\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-12,2]\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nt\\in [-12,2].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625927 :question/problem "The inequality $-4\\le t\\le -2$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-4$ and $-2$ *are* included. \n\n$$\n-4\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,t\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-4,-2]\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nt\\in [-4,-2].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625937 :question/problem "The inequality $-11\\le t\\le 6$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-11$ and $6$ *are* included. \n\n$$\n-11\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,t\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,6\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-11,6]\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nt\\in [-11,6].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625946 :question/problem "The inequality $2 \\le x < 14$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given inequality\n\n$$\n2 \\le x < 14\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $2$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $[$.\n- The right endpoint $14$ *is not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $)$.\n\nTherefore, the interval that corresponds to the given inequality is\n\n$$\nx ∈ [2, 14)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625955 :question/problem "The interval $u ∈ (-2, -1]$ corresponds to the inequality" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given interval\n\n$$\nu ∈ (-2, -1]\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-2$ *is not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $<$.\n- The right endpoint $-1$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $\\le$.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is\n\n$$\n-2 < u \\le -1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625956 :question/problem "The interval $x ∈ [0, 15)$ corresponds to the inequality" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given interval\n\n$$\nx ∈ [0, 15)\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $0$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $\\le$.\n- The right endpoint $15$ *is not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $<$.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is\n\n$$\n0 \\le x < 15\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625971 :question/problem "The interval $t ∈ [-9, 13)$ corresponds to the inequality" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given interval\n\n$$\nt ∈ [-9, 13)\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-9$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $\\le$.\n- The right endpoint $13$ *is not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $<$.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is\n\n$$\n-9 \\le t < 13\n$$"}]}
